<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Saffarid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">861 CE – 1003 CE · capital Zaranj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty founded by a coppersmith from Sistan that briefly conquered much of Iran and helped revive Persian as a literary language.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth</a>, a coppersmith (saffar) turned leader of the ayyar volunteer bands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a> in 861 while fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>. He rapidly conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid dynasty</a> by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in 873 and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub</a>'s state rested on a professional army and on personal leadership rather than caliphal appointment. He is said to have objected to praise in Arabic, which he did not understand, encouraging poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-wasif">Muhammad ibn Wasif</a> to compose in Persian, an early step in the New Persian literary revival. Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dayr-al-aqul">Dayr al-Aqul</a> in 876, he died in 879. His brother Amr ibn al-Layth was recognised as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Amr was defeated and captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> ruler Ismail at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in 900. A reduced Saffarid line held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaf-ibn-ahmad">Khalaf ibn Ahmad</a> surrendered to Mahmud of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> in 1003; later Saffarid descendants ruled as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nasrid-maliks-of-sistan">Nasrid Maliks of Sistan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saffarid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Saffarid dynasty" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Saffarid_dynasty_(861%E2%80%931002).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>861 CE – 1003 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaranj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ya'qub ibn al-Layth, Amr ibn al-Layth, Khalaf ibn Ahmad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saffarids, Saffarid emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=saffarid-dynasty" aria-label="Saffarid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6280 1049 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saffarid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6600 1305L6480 1127M6600 1305L6134 1224M6600 1305L6588 1010M6600 1305L6748 1217M6600 1305L6600 1305"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6480 1127h0M6134 1224h0M6588 1010h0M6748 1217h0M6600 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6600 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6600 1305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zaranj. <a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=saffarid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
